WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:03.827
Many visitors already have a Microsoft account.

00:00:03.827 --> 00:00:08.629
The Microsoft Account Authentication feature lets them sign in to your site with it.

00:00:08.629 --> 00:00:12.736
In this video, you enable the feature, enter its settings, and find the

00:00:12.736 --> 00:00:14.390
button on the login page.

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:21.920
The feature adds Microsoft as a way to log in.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:25.864
A visitor chooses it on your login page, signs in on a Microsoft

00:00:25.864 --> 00:00:28.230
page, and is sent back to your site.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:33.026
You create an app for your site in the Microsoft developer portal, which

00:00:33.026 --> 00:00:36.501
gives you an Application ID and lets you create a secret key.

00:00:36.501 --> 00:00:40.702
The app also needs to use your site's callback path, where visitors return.

00:00:40.702 --> 00:00:42.880
The settings page shows the default.

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:46.501
This feature covers Microsoft accounts.

00:00:46.501 --> 00:00:52.242
For work or school accounts as well, the documentation recommends Microsoft Entra ID

00:00:52.242 --> 00:00:57.090
authentication with a multi-tenant app, which a separate video covers.

00:01:01.382 --> 00:01:05.394
Let's open Tools, then Features, and search for Microsoft Account.

00:01:05.394 --> 00:01:11.524
The Microsoft Account Authentication feature appears under the Microsoft Authentication category, with a

00:01:11.524 --> 00:01:14.032
short description and an Enable button.

00:01:14.507 --> 00:01:15.744
Let's click Enable.

00:01:15.744 --> 00:01:20.446
The feature depends on External Authentication, which Orchard Core enables along with it,

00:01:20.446 --> 00:01:23.317
and a message confirms that the feature was enabled.

00:01:27.617 --> 00:01:31.810
Let's open Settings, then Security, Authentication, and Microsoft.

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:36.237
The page starts with a warning that the website might restart when you

00:01:36.237 --> 00:01:39.207
save, which can make it briefly unresponsive.

00:01:39.688 --> 00:01:44.107
AppId is the Application ID of the app you created at Microsoft, and

00:01:44.107 --> 00:01:46.616
the form cannot be saved without it.

00:01:46.616 --> 00:01:48.348
Let's type a sample ID.

00:01:48.813 --> 00:01:52.096
Secret Key holds the secret you created for the app.

00:01:52.096 --> 00:01:57.473
The field hides what you type, and Orchard Core stores the saved value in protected form.

00:01:57.937 --> 00:01:59.767
CallbackPath is optional.

00:01:59.767 --> 00:02:04.252
Leave it empty to use the default path, shown here as the placeholder,

00:02:04.252 --> 00:02:07.557
and set the Microsoft app up to use the same path.

00:02:08.014 --> 00:02:12.728
Store tokens saves the access and refresh tokens from the sign-in for reuse.

00:02:12.728 --> 00:02:14.394
Let's leave it cleared.

00:02:14.888 --> 00:02:16.260
Let's click Save.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:22.284
A message confirms that the site settings were updated, and the website restarts to apply them.

00:02:22.284 --> 00:02:26.578
The login button appears once AppId and Secret Key are both saved.

00:02:30.847 --> 00:02:33.493
Let's visit the login page as a visitor.

00:02:33.493 --> 00:02:37.692
Beside the Log in form, a section titled Use another service to log

00:02:37.692 --> 00:02:40.107
in shows a Microsoft Account button.

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:44.418
Choosing the button sends the visitor to Microsoft to sign in.

00:02:44.418 --> 00:02:50.180
Let's stop here, because the sign-in itself happens on Microsoft's side, with the app you created.

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:59.066
When Microsoft sends the visitor back, Orchard Core looks for a local user

00:02:59.066 --> 00:03:01.819
already linked to that Microsoft account.

00:03:01.819 --> 00:03:04.630
If it finds one, the visitor is signed in.

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:09.331
If no link exists but a local user has the same email address,

00:03:09.331 --> 00:03:14.910
the visitor sees Link your account, and enters that user's local password to link the two.

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:22.727
Otherwise, the visitor sees Create a new account, with Username, Email, Password and Confirm password fields.

00:03:22.727 --> 00:03:28.073
The external registration settings can turn new registrations off or skip some of

00:03:28.073 --> 00:03:31.330
those fields, and a separate video covers them.

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:38.270
Signed-in users can also link a Microsoft account later, from External Logins in the user menu.

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:46.058
The Microsoft Account Authentication feature adds a Microsoft Account button to your login

00:03:46.058 --> 00:03:48.952
page, once AppId and Secret Key are saved.

00:03:48.952 --> 00:03:50.520
Thanks for watching.

